# Security rules

## Never do these

- Never add cloud servers, relay services, or internet fallbacks
- Never persist keys, secrets, or session data to disk or local storage
- Never bypass the receiver approval step
- Never allow more than 2 participants
- Never put a secret in a QR code or a pairing code
- Never disable or weaken AEAD, and never reuse a nonce
- Never reuse an ephemeral key across sessions
- Never trust an mDNS TXT record as a public key. Only the QR code pins a key. Typed codes rely on the verification number

## Always do these

- Encrypt with XChaCha20-Poly1305 and a fresh 24-byte nonce
- Derive keys with HKDF-SHA256 over the exact handshake bytes
- Generate a new X25519 key pair for every code
- Check the protocol version and message type on every frame
- Require the next sequence number, and bind it as AEAD associated data
- Wipe session keys on close with `SecretKeyData.destroy()` (`overwriteWhenDestroyed: true`)
- Compare pinned public keys in constant time

## Crypto

| Operation | Primitive | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Key agreement | X25519 | `lib/core/crypto.dart` |
| Derivation | HKDF-SHA256 | `lib/core/crypto.dart` |
| Encryption | XChaCha20-Poly1305 | `lib/core/crypto.dart` |
| Nonce | 24 random bytes | `cryptography` |
| Wipe | overwrite on destroy for AEAD keys | `SecretKeyData` |

`cryptography_flutter` may call the operating system for the same algorithms. Do not add a second crypto library. The X25519 private key wipe is best-effort. See `docs/THREAT_MODEL.md`.
